{
 "type": "FeatureCollection",
 "metadata": {
  "note": "Rail transit in Portland, Oregon. MAX opened on 5 September 1986 as the Banfield light rail line and grew into a five-line network: Westside to Hillsboro in 1998, the airport Red Line in 2001, the Interstate Avenue Yellow Line in 2004, the I-205 Green Line in 2009 and the Orange Line to Milwaukie in 2015 over Tilikum Crossing, a bridge that carries transit, bikes and pedestrians but no cars. All five MAX lines share one TriMet colour under Rule 4. The Portland Streetcar, which opened on 20 July 2001, was the first modern streetcar system built in the United States; it is owned by the City of Portland and operated under contract, and is treated here as a separate company with its own colour and tagged streetcar rather than light_rail.",
  "sources": [
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/MAX_Light_Rail (MAX opening dates line by line, 1986 through 2015)",
   "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portland_Streetcar (2001 opening, 2012 Central Loop)",
   "OpenStreetMap route relations, extracted 29 August 2026; traced length is compared with the documented route length on each feature."
  ],
  "not_yet_sourced": "Headways and speeds are estimates; no TriMet timetable was consulted. Each MAX line is drawn at its present extent for its whole period, so pre-extension years show more track than existed — the Blue Line in 1986–1998 and the Red Line before its 2024 Hillsboro extension in particular. The Green Line feature draws only its own I-205 branch. Whether Portland Streetcar should be one company with TriMet under Rule 4 is genuinely unclear: OSM tags its operator as TriMet, but the system is owned by the City of Portland and branded separately. Treated as separate here; worth a second look."
 },
